A discount. An outbound note to a live account. A change to the number you will say on the forecast call. Careful teams already pause these. Someone is supposed to say yes.

The failure mode is not the pause. The failure mode is a pause that lives in a chat thread. Next week the pipeline review cannot say which rule was used, what the odds were, or who released the work.

## Write the gate you already believe in

Breaker, in The Circuit Method™, is that gate. Written gates, plus a named person's recorded approval, stand before anything reaches a customer or moves money. The receipt names the rule, the odds at the time, who approved, and what went out.

Internal chores can sit at a lighter standard. Changing a field the customer will never see is not the same act as sending the note. Say so in the table, or the team will invent the line under pressure.

## What a model may prepare

A model can draft the note, attach the row that matched, and put the package in a queue. That is current. It is not the yes.

If the same tool can also release the package, you do not have a breaker. You have a suggestion that learned to send.

## The week after

The pipeline review should be able to start from receipts, not from memory. Which drafts were released. Which were held. Which rules fired. Where the odds and the later outcome disagreed.

That disagreement is not an embarrassment to hide. It is ground. It is how the next forecast call gets more honest, and how a routing rule earns the right to stay on the page.

Skip the gate and you have a short circuit. The work moved. The reason did not survive until Thursday.
